        "# Assignment 1 S&P 500 (1998–2025):\n",
        "\n",
        "**OPIM 5641 · Business Decision Modeling**\n",
        "**Robert Staniszewski**\n",
        "\n",
        "**A note on this dataset choice:**\n",
        "For my dataset. I pulled real daily S&P 500 prices straight from Yahoo Finance using the yfinance Python package found from google, covering 1998- my birthyear to 2025. This is different from the annual returns file we used in class mine is live data I grabbed myself just from yahoo finance, it's daily instead of yearly. It covers a different time range, and I used it to answer a different question: what a lump sum invested today might be worth one year from now, instead of a 35-year retirement savings plan.\n",
        "\n",
        "**The two halves, and how they connect:**\n",
        "\n",
        "I looked closely at the daily price data: checking for missing/bad values, seeing how far the market dropped during the internet crash in 2000, the 2008 financial crisis, and COVID, and then pulling in real inflation data so that I could tell how much of the market's growth was real versus just prices going up everywhere.\n",
        "\n",
        "Simulating what I don't have, nobody actually knows what the index will be worth exactly one year from now, unless you work in the government. So I took the real daily ups and downs from my own data crashes and all and used them to run 10,000 simulated \"what if\" years, to get a probability of different outcomes instead of just guessing one number.\n"
