      "source": [
        "#9 Interpretation, limitations &  takeaway\n",
        "\n",
        "the risk comes in waves and \"extreme days happen more than expected\" findings from sections 4 and 5 aren't just interesting side facts, they're the actual reason my two simulations in section 8 gave different answers. A model that only knows the market's average return and average risk is throwing away real information, like how clustered the risk is and how big the real crashes actually were, that matters a lot to someone deciding how much risk they're okay with.\n",
        "\n",
        "Being honest about the limits of this\n",
        "\n",
        "My stock data doesn't include dividends, just the price. A real investor also earns dividends on top of price growth (historically around 1.5 to 2% a year), so both my historical growth numbers and my simulated results are a bit lower than what someone would actually earn in real life.\n",
        "My inflation comparison in section 6 only used two data points (start and end), not a full month by month breakdown. That's fine for getting a rough sense of the inflation effect, but it's not a precise calculation.\n",
        "My simulation treats each day as random and unconnected to the day before it. In real markets, a bad day makes another bad day slightly more likely\n"
      ],
      "id": "_AOPEl78YmRF"
